WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.765
RTIC systems often interact with hardware
through interrupt handlers,

2
00:00:03.845 --> 00:00:07.699
peripheral access crates, hardware
abstraction layers, memory-mapped registers,

3
00:00:07.779 --> 00:00:09.759
DMA engines, and device-specific drivers.

4
00:00:09.839 --> 00:00:13.479
These interfaces connect Rust code to
behavior the compiler cannot fully model.

5
00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:17.145
Peripheral ownership should make it hard
for unrelated code to control the same

6
00:00:17.225 --> 00:00:18.349
hardware unexpectedly.

7
00:00:18.429 --> 00:00:22.069
Initialization order, clock state, pin
configuration, interrupt enablement,

8
00:00:22.149 --> 00:00:24.878
and reset behavior can all affect security
and reliability.

9
00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:27.581
The architecture should make those
assumptions visible.

10
00:00:27.661 --> 00:00:30.337
Memory-mapped I/O has side effects outside
normal memory.

11
00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:33.896
Reads and writes may acknowledge
interrupts, start transfers, clear flags,

12
00:00:33.976 --> 00:00:35.154
or change hardware state.

13
00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:39.088
Safe abstractions should guide callers
toward valid sequences and prevent ordinary

14
00:00:39.168 --> 00:00:41.576
code from violating important hardware
invariants.

15
00:00:41.656 --> 00:00:45.349
DMA requires special care because hardware
may read from or write to buffers while

16
00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:46.714
software also wants access.

17
00:00:46.794 --> 00:00:49.470
The firmware should define who owns a
buffer at each moment,

18
00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.440
when it is valid to read or modify it, how
completion is signaled,

19
00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.875
and what happens if a transfer is
cancelled or fails.

20
00:00:54.955 --> 00:00:58.435
Unsafe hardware access should be small,
justified, documented, and tested.

21
00:00:58.515 --> 00:01:01.940
A safety contract should explain pointer
validity, register assumptions,

22
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.285
aliasing expectations, interrupt
interactions, and ownership rules.

23
00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:09.058
Safe wrappers should protect callers from
needing to remember those assumptions

24
00:01:09.138 --> 00:01:09.620
manually.
